# The engine's plan · `plan:nrz:engine-plan`

## Order and dependencies · `sec:nrz:plan-order-and-dependencies`

The execution order is documentation generator, exemplar register, numerical feasibility, units design, units and numerical foundations, certified matrix core, initial physics core, advanced matrix domains, and accuracy and benchmarks. The exemplar-register bite is numbered 1b between bites 1 and 2. This order realises the architectural sequencing and mapping while supplying reference targets before numerical experiments (`dec:vision:sequencing-adjustment`), (`tab:vision:bite-mapping`), (`dec:exemplars:exemplar-bite`).

Documentation generation supports every subsequent bite. The exemplar seeds and adversarial fixtures precede feasibility; feasibility constrains the units decision; that decision and validated scalar contracts precede the foundations; the foundations support the certified matrix core. Physics uses only supported matrix capabilities. Advanced domains require their own certificates before dependent physics capabilities expand. Consolidation evaluates the capabilities established by the preceding bites. The units-design and foundations bites use the nrz-algebra original-tree, exact-rational, collection and parser contract; nrz-units supplies data-time dimension, measure, kind, count and uncertainty checking over it (`req:vision:expression-and-symbol-contract`). The reusable substrate and the obligations of the existing bites are separated by (`dec:nrz:algebra-and-data-time-units-boundary`), without adding or renumbering a bite. The physical vocabulary is the architecture’s dimensions, measures, and counts, with semantic kinds and affine roles enforced in addition to dimension algebra (`def:vision:dimensions`), (`def:vision:measures`), (`def:vision:counts`), (`rule:vision:semantic-kinds-and-roles`), (`req:vision:algebraic-unit-checks`). Measurement and computational uncertainties are intrinsic matrix contents with their own algebraically derived descriptors (`def:vision:intrinsic-uncertainty-companions`), (`rule:vision:uncertainty-unit-algebra`).

Every numerical bite carries its own accuracy tests and representative measurements. The final stage broadens that evidence. Each bite declares its supported domain, assurance, failure behavior, and exclusions; failure to establish a required guarantee either narrows the explicitly supported deliverable or leaves the bite incomplete (`rule:vision:verification-is-continuous`), (`rule:vision:completion-boundaries`). A cited exemplar contributes its stated take and refusal (`rule:exemplars:take-and-refuse`).

**Decision (Algebra and data-time units boundary)** · `dec:nrz:algebra-and-data-time-units-boundary`

The reusable substrate supplies bounded original expressions, exact rational arithmetic, deterministic non-expanding collection, substitution and a declared text grammar in nrz-algebra. The nrz-units client supplies authoritative descriptors, semantic rules, count refusals, independent uncertainty relationships and original-expression checks before normalization (`req:vision:expression-and-symbol-contract`), (`dec:vision:data-time-unit-descriptors`), (`rule:vision:check-before-normalization`). Descriptor representation and the typed computation boundary are selected by (`dec:nrz:descriptor-unit-authority`), with an integral const-generic comparison and the scope of (`cav:nrz:descriptor-prototype-scope`). Foundations still owns production numerical integration, conversion enclosures, discrete storage and uncertainty propagation; matrix and physics promote checked storage, publication and plans through their own boundaries (`dec:nrz:descriptor-prototype-promotion`). The versioned problem-data schema and migration are a separate next lane under (`req:vision:unit-data-migration`), implemented by the units/docgen owners through foundations work. Legacy problem strings and bare numerical packages acquire no algebraic proof from the prototype.

**Decision (Shape model implementation boundary)** · `dec:nrz:shape-model-implementation-boundary`

The shape specification supplies the logical operator, companion and evidence contracts before numerical matrix integration (`spec:nrz:matrix-shapes`). It leaves scalar algebra physics-free and extends the data-time units boundary through ordered entry identities and contractions (`rule:nrz:shape-data-time-evidence`). The computation specification shares checked shapes while retaining independent value and source identities (`spec:nrz:descriptor-heap-computation`), (`dec:nrz:shape-descriptor-ownership`). The descriptor prototype exercises a limited dense shape and paired covariance subset (`ex:nrz:descriptor-resistor-prototype`). Production shape descriptors, matrix operations, tensor uncertainty propagation, statistical validation and data migration remain implementing work; the specification and documentation notices establish no additional runtime support. The existing bite order and measured feasibility facts remain unchanged. Shape acceptance and refusal evidence is required by (`req:nrz:shape-implementation-evidence`).

**Decision (Computation model implementation boundary)** · `dec:nrz:computation-model-implementation-boundary`

The computation specification defines the descriptor heap, plans, bindings and batches before their implementation (`spec:nrz:descriptor-heap-computation`). The matrix layer owns safe arenas, handles, views and evidence invalidation; physics owns computational plans over that public boundary (`dec:nrz:heap-ownership-and-sharing`). Units supplies reusable original-expression and declaration evidence without depending on storage, and algebra remains generic. The decision prototype exercises the selected typed boundary for one resistor recipe (`def:nrz:checked-descriptor-boundary`). Production owners rewrite its toy storage and execution while retaining its invariant tests (`dec:nrz:descriptor-prototype-promotion`). The existing bites implement these obligations in their present order, without changing measured feasibility facts or claiming production support from documentation notices. Acceptance requires (`req:nrz:computation-implementation-evidence`); hardware targeting and parallel scheduling are outside this contract.

